WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.250
Afghanistan is a difficult country…

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:11.860
In many aspects.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.460
And “difficult” honestly doesn’t really
cover it.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:18.540
Afghanistan is a very mountainous country
- almost three quarters of its surface is

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.840
covered by mountains and peaks that in many
cases exceed 20,000ft.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.060
So, you can probably imagine just how difficult
communications are among the different regions

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:27.060
of this country.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.810
And as if this was not enough, it’s also
a landlocked and mostly arid place.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:38.770
In fact, deserts are not unusual in Afghanistan.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:41.610
So with the exception of few valleys like
Herat’s, its national landscape are mainly

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.750
composed of red and yellow tones.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:48.890
BUT, geography is not the worst part of this
country, neither is its harsh climate conditions...

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:49.890
not at all.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:55.150
Poverty, corruption, and especially fundamentalism
and violence are the worst part of this place.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.970
Because, dear viewers, Afghanistan is above
all a country of war.

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:05.100
In 1978 the Saur Revolution, a communist based
revolution, took place.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:11.490
One year later, in 1979, the Soviet Army invaded
the country to consolidate the communist government.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:18.569
Since then, Afghans have not known what peace
means.

00:01:18.569 --> 00:01:22.189
But before, to be honest, the situation wasn’t
really good either.

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:26.520
Afghanistan was, is and probably will be for
a long time, one of the poorest countries

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:32.710
on Earth, with one of the hardest and most
hostile living conditions - especially for

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:34.490
women.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.929
In this country 6 of every 10 people don’t
know how to read or write, almost half of

00:01:37.929 --> 00:01:42.090
its population doesn’t have access to drinking
water, and it’s difficult to find a single

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.500
family which hasn’t been devastated by war
and violence.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.170
We can say safely that if someone was able
to choose where they were to be born…

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:51.600
No one would ever choose Afghanistan…

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.149
The last great conflict (which still persists
to this day) burst onto the global stage in

00:01:56.149 --> 00:02:01.939
October 2001, when the President of the United
States, George W. Bush, decided to invade

00:02:01.939 --> 00:02:04.079
Afghanistan as a response to 9-11.

00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:08.500
By that time Afghanistan was led by the Taliban,
a radical religious-political group which

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:20.870
protected members of Al-Qaeda - Bin Laden
among them.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:27.680
The invasion was fast - the Taliban simply
weren’t an obstacle for the massive American

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:28.680
war machine.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.730
16 years and 3 Presidents later though and
it has been named by many the longest war

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.620
in US history - it’s already longer than
the war in Vietnam.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:37.620
And...

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:42.620
Believe it or not, the US is not “winning”

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:48.060
(WHAT’S HAPPENING?)

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.260
Sixteen long years of conflict, occupation,
and hundreds of billions of dollars later

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.980
and it would seem logical to think that the
US and NATO could have managed to have the

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:59.950
situation under control and Afghanistan would
be close to reaching peace, right?

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:00.950
Well...

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:01.950
Nothing is closer to reality.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:05.050
The truth is that each day that goes by, the
Taliban regain ground.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:09.180
Take a look to this map from the Washington
Post: the colored areas are the ones controlled

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:14.490
by the Taliban:
And well, this map shows the situation in

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:15.500
the beginning of 2017.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.420
But today things are even worse, because as
we just said the Taliban are regaining ground

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.340
almost every day.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:28.790
The latest estimates show that the government
of Kabul, the US, and NATO control less than

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.290
60% of the territory.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:34.460
And the next map is going to show even more
Taliban controlled regions:

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:39.650
”Afghan government controls just 57 percent
of its territory, U.S. watchdog says”

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:43.840
But the loss of control by the Afghan government,
and thus the US, shouldn’t just been seen

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.320
as territorial loss, but also economic loss.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:49.720
For example, we can ask ourselves…

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.310
How can the Taliban finance their activity?

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.510
Where do all their resources come from?

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.120
Well, basically, that would be from heroin
production.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.950
Yep, as you might know.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:03.880
Afghanistan is the biggest opium producer
in the world, and this... this is the raw

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.750
ingredient used for heroin.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:11.290
And, listen, Afghanistan is not just the world’s
biggest producer, it controls between 80 and

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.569
90% of opium production globally!

00:04:15.569 --> 00:04:20.630
The opium comes from papaver somniferum, the
most popular plant among the Afghan farmers.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:25.730
In Afghanistan most of its population live
of farming.

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:31.130
We can say that most of the Afghan economy
relies on two fields: international soldiers

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:32.130
and opium.

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.150
In fact, there’s little more than that.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:38.330
So, for quite a while the Taliban have controlled
the opium production in this country.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:42.750
But now, also, they have realised that they
can produce the heroin themselves, which is

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.540
a much more profitable activity.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:47.260
And, well, that’s exactly what they are
doing:

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:50.290
"Everything they harvest is duly processed
inside the country.

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:54.330
They receive more revenues if they process
opium before it has left the country".

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:58.570
William Brownfield, US Assistant Secretary
for Drugs and Law Enforcement.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:02.720
Estimates show that the Taliban could be raising
around £3 billion every year from this.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.680
And that’s despite all the money that the
US has wasted trying to stop these activities.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:17.169
An effort that, as you can see from this chart,
hasn’t been helpful:

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:21.090
So despite all the effort made by the US,
opium production remains at very high levels.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:25.419
OK, so as a consequence of having all these
resources, the Taliban are managing to sustain

00:05:25.419 --> 00:05:30.070
a war that is not just allowing them to regain
ground, but also has plunged the country into

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.070
violence.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:34.180
And worst of all is that predictions are not
good at all:

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:38.270
“The Taliban are getting stronger, the government
is on the retreat, they are losing ground

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:42.520
to the Taliban day by day,” Abdul Jabbar
Qahraman, a retired general.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:47.260
Listen to this: the Taliban “army” has
increased from between 2,000 to 10,000 soldiers

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.920
in 2005 to more than 60,000 today.”

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.290
But, at this point, the question is…

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.419
Why is the US losing this war?

00:05:54.419 --> 00:06:02.740
Or, at least, why are they not winning?

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:06.050
(THE AFGHAN TRAP)

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:11.520
The United States has tried many different
strategies in Afghanistan, but it seems that

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:12.520
nothing works.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:16.139
In fact, generally speaking, there’s quite
a lot of pessimism over this conflict.

00:06:16.139 --> 00:06:19.310
And this is not just a matter of more or less
soldiers.

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:24.370
When Barack Obama was a candidate for president
he repeated all the time that Afghanistan

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:26.541
was “a war they need to win”:

00:06:26.541 --> 00:06:30.310
We did not finish the job against al Qaeda
in Afghanistan.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:34.860
We did not develop new capabilities to defeat
a new enemy, or launch a comprehensive strategy

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.000
to dry up the terrorists' base of support.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:39.840
We did not reaffirm our basic values, or secure
our homeland.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:40.970
It is time to turn the page.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:44.000
When I am President, we will wage the war
that has to be won (..).

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.000
Barack Obama, 1st August 2007

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:50.270
When he arrived to the White House he deployed
100,000 soldiers and another 40,000 from NATO.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:51.900
Even so, nothing changed.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:56.030
Many experts say that the problem is that
President Obama insisted all the time on an

00:06:56.030 --> 00:07:00.370
exit plan with a “troop withdrawal calendar”,
which is sort of like saying to the Taliban…

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:03.240
“Hey, look, don’t worry, all of this is
temporary”.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:07.270
But, beyond that, although the soldiers from
the international coalition gained ground,

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.750
they couldn’t gain influence there.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.240
So why is that?

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:15.900
Well, first of all, we are talking about a
tribal country, with a culture of consolidated

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:16.900
violence.

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:20.710
It’s not easy for international troops to
gain people’s trust and be able to distinguish

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:24.560
civilians from enemies, or even just know
what’s happening on the ground.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:30.080
The cultural leap is HUGE, and having suffered
so many years of violence means that the Taliban

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:31.870
have plenty of experience…

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:36.020
On the other hand, there’s the corruption
and the lack of credibility in the local government.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:41.130
We can say safely that the Afghan government
lacks of institutions which can provide anything

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:42.350
to the Afghan people.

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:46.229
And the few which exist are extremely corrupt.

00:07:46.229 --> 00:07:49.580
In fact, this is one of the most corrupt countries
on Earth:

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:51.870
“Ordinary Afghans don’t like the Taliban.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:54.370
But they dislike the Afghan government even
more.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:56.620
&nbsp;[...] People don’t die for an army; they
die for a country.

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:59.180
And who wants to die for the current Afghan
government?”

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:01.330
Dexter Filkins, New Yorker reporter.

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:06.479
With all of this and the fear to the Taliban
is easy to understand why every month, EVERY

00:08:06.479 --> 00:08:10.990
MONTH, almost 4,000 soldiers and policemen
desert the government of Kabul.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:14.889
Further, we have to say that the strategy
the US has taken with the Afghan government,

00:08:14.889 --> 00:08:17.960
the politics and the institutions, it hasn’t
worked…

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:22.199
Washington hasn’t even made the Afghan military
able to function, with the exception of the

00:08:22.199 --> 00:08:27.380
special forces, which is formed of 17,000
elite soldiers - they are the only ones who

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:28.860
are really fighting the Taliban.

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:33.789
Barack Obama understood that the war in Afghanistan
was something that Afghan people should carry

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:49.490
out, though the US would be the one paying
the bills…

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:53.320
Among all the problems, we have also have
to add in: one, Pakistan who is being accused

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:58.060
of providing support to the Taliban, and two,
an economy which doesn’t work at all, and

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:00.820
then three, a lot of concealed interests.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:05.110
For example, have you thought about what interests
Beijing, Tehran or Moscow have in this war?

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:09.630
One thing is clear here, these countries doesn’t
want Afghanistan to be an American “colony”.

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:12.760
Because of all of this, after 16 years of
war, the battle continues.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:21.630
But now, at the Oval Office there’s a new
President: Donald Trump...

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:27.550
(A HUGE MISTAKE?)

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:31.450
Trump has been saying for years that the war
on Afghanistan is a huge mistake, and that

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:33.220
the US has to get out of there.

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:37.210
He said it, of course, though his favorite
communication medium: Twitter.

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:51.240
He repeated it again and again: in 2011, 2012,
2013… all the way until his 2016 campaign.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:55.870
It is time to get out of Afghanistan.

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:58.420
We are building roads and schools for people
that hate us.

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:00.960
It is not in our national interests.

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:06.810
Do not allow our very stupid leaders to sign
a deal that keeps us in Afghanistan through

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:08.580
2024-with all costs by U.S.A.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:09.580
MAKE AMERICA GREAT!

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:11.060
Of course he didn’t miss an opportunity
to mess with Obama:

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:14.450
Now Obama is keeping our soldiers in Afghanistan
for at least another year.

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:15.820
He is losing two wars simultaneously.

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:27.910
But it wasn’t just Twitter, Trump insisted
again and again: Afghanistan was a mistake.

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:32.620
LOOK:

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:40.640
And of course, in the presidential campaign
he promised he will get the US out of Afghanistan

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:41.640
if elected.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:42.640
BUT…

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:43.640
Now that he’s President…

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:48.130
Well, things seem to be quite different…

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:53.550
TRUMP’S NEW POSITION

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:57.820
On the 21st August, in the military base of
Fort Myer in Virginia, Trump explained his

00:10:57.820 --> 00:10:59.660
brand new strategy, and well…

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:02.400
Let’s just say that everything he’s been
saying for years…

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:03.910
Came to nothing.

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:06.600
Trump’s new strategy is so similar to Obama’s
that…

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:08.970
It’s really difficult to differentiate them.

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.600
Trump announced more troops, but not that
many more...

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:15.510
Basically, the contingent will increase from
12,000 to around 16,000.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:19.480
The training of the Afghan military and the
fight against terrorism will become a priority.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:23.220
More money will be spent, corruption will
be battled, and there will be more pressure

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:24.220
on Pakistan

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:25.850
So, in other words, more of the same.

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:27.810
Nothing that the US hadn’t done before.

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:32.959
The main differences from Obama’s strategy
are twofold: first there won’t be a withdrawal

00:11:32.959 --> 00:11:37.910
calendar, and second Trump seems to not be
interested in building Afghan institutions.

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:41.950
In fact, with the budget cuts on the State
Department, Trump seems to rely more on arms

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:42.950
than politics.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:46.960
And, it’s not yet clear what Trump plans
to do with 16,000 soldiers what Obama couldn’t

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:53.000
do with 140,000, or how he thinks he can stabilize
a country without solid and reliable institutions

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:56.450
which could gain local people’s trust and
allow some economic development.

00:11:56.450 --> 00:12:01.640
The average age in Afghanistan is around 18
years old, which is a really young population,

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:05.779
and this youth nowadays can’t work and doesn’t
have any other future apart from what the

00:12:05.779 --> 00:12:07.060
Taliban can offer them.

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:11.470
If Kabul or Washington are not able to build
a framework of opportunities for these people…

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:14.120
The end of violence will be far away.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:20.810
So the question must be: Is Donald Trump aware
of this?

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:23.690
America First, America First.

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:30.410
So, 16 years, more than $800 billion, and
thousands and thousands of military and civilian

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:35.769
losses later and United States is still bogged
down in what many call the Afghan Trap.

00:12:35.769 --> 00:12:39.160
And the truth is that Trump doesn’t seem
to propose anything different.

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:43.850
Without institutions and without a multilateral
willingness which must include Afghanistan's

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:48.810
neighbours, like China, it seems very hard
to believe that weapons are going to be enough.

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:52.610
But now it is your turn: what should the US
do in Afghanistan?

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:55.120
